  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1878,
    "summary": "Every use still costs a radio round trip, sent right after the unlock while the lock may be busy. The provider already keeps `_slot_by_user_index`, a map from lock user index to slot that every read and write refreshes and that `slot_tag` reads. That map could answer most uses with no read at all.",
    "failure_scenario": "On the reporter's Aqara U400, one `GetUser` took about 6 s because the lock was re-locking. The single `GetUser` now goes out at exactly that moment, so the event can still arrive several seconds late and automations still miss the name. Answering from the map first and reading only when the index isn't there would remove the round trip in the common case."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1906,
    "summary": "The LockOperation read (`_lock_operation_slot`) runs outside the worker's turn (`coordinator.async_in_turn`), while the LockUserChange read in the same PR does run in turn, with the comment 'nothing reaches it once its worker stops'.",
    "failure_scenario": "A keypad use arrives while the worker has stopped or is backing off, or while a SetCredential is in flight. LCM sends `GetUser` straight to the lock anyway, which bypasses the worker's backoff and puts a second command in front of the lock alongside the write."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 535,
    "summary": "The single read skips `_ensure_usr_support`, which `get_lock_users` calls. On a lock without user management, the PIN-index lookup now fails as a retryable `LockDisconnected` instead of a permanent `LockOperationFailed`.",
