content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I found 9 issues in PR 1745: 6 correctness and 3 cleanup/conventions. Nothing was verified, as this pass skips verification, so treat these as candidates. I left the Zigbee2MQTT `action_source_name` strings (`rfid`, `fingerprint`) unchecked because no zigbee-herdsman-converters checkout is available here.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 258,
    "summary": "The Keypad-source branch returns PIN before the credentialType is looked at, so a ProgrammingPIN (credentialType 0, the lock's admin code) entered at the keypad is now reported as a user's PIN use; before this PR it was ignored.",
    "failure_scenario": "A LockOperation arrives with operationSource=3 (Keypad), credentials=[{credentialType: 0, credentialIndex: 1}], and a userIndex that resolves to an LCM-tagged user (for example user 1). `_kind_used` returns PIN, `used` is non-empty, and the userIndex path fires credential_used. The Slot Usage Limiter then spends a use for an admin-code entry. The `_MATTER_TO_CREDENTIAL_TYPE` comment says ProgrammingPIN is 'not reported', but that only holds for the Remote and Unspecified sources."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 265,
    "summary": "A Biometric source only reports when credentialType maps to fingerprint or face. On the firmware this PR is built around (it writes operationSource into credentialType), a biometric use arrives with credentialType 9, which is not a CredentialTypeEnum value, so it is dropped.",
    "failure_scenario": "A U400-style lock sends operationSource=9 (Biometric) with credentials=[{credentialType: 9, credentialIndex: 2}] and a valid userIndex. `_MATTER_TO_CREDENTIAL_TYPE.get(9)` is None, the Biometric guard returns None, and the fingerprint use is never reported. Keypad and RFID uses on the same firmware are rescued by the source. A Biometric source with an unknown type should fall back to FINGERPRINT."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 121,
    "summary": "Removed behavior: a credential the lock typed as PIN (credentialType 1) used to be reported from any operationSource. It is now dropped under Manual, ProprietaryRemote, Auto, Button, Schedule, Aliro and Biometric, and under the Rfid source it is reported as RFID and is no longer placed by its PIN index.",
    "failure_scenario": "A spec-following lock reports a PIN entry with operationSource=5 (Button) or 1 (Manual) and credentials=[{credentialType: 1, credentialIndex: 4}]. Before this PR that was a PIN use for the user. Now `_kind_used` returns None and the use is silently lost. Separately, a card-plus-PIN unlock under source Rfid with no userIndex used to be placed through PIN index 4 and is now dropped, because `placeable_by_pin_index` is False."
  },
  {
    "file": "blueprints/automation/lock_code_manager/slot_usage_limiter.yaml",
    "line": 24,
    "summary": "Card and fingerprint uses now spend the slot's limit, but when the counter reaches 0 the limiter turns off the slot. That clears only the PIN Lock Code Manager manages; a card or fingerprint enrolled in the vendor app keeps working.",
    "failure_scenario": "A Matter user has an LCM PIN and a vendor-enrolled card, with a limit of 3. Three card taps (now reported as `rfid`, where before this PR Matter dropped them) bring the counter to 0. The switch turns off and the PIN is cleared, so the guest loses a PIN they never used, while the card unlocks indefinitely. The limit is spent by a credential it cannot revoke."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1895,
    "summary": "A missing or unparseable credentialIndex aborts the use even when userIndex is present, although the index is only needed for the PIN fallback. Now that RFID, fingerprint and face uses resolve through userIndex, they are dropped for no reason.",
    "failure_scenario": "A lock sends operationSource=8 (Rfid), userIndex=3 (an LCM-tagged user), and credentials=[{credentialType: 2, credentialIndex: null}] or a non-numeric index. `parse_slot_num` returns None and the handler returns before dispatch. The same happens when the PIN-typed credential (picked first) has a bad index and a second credential is fine."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/event.py",
    "line": 222,
    "summary": "Root-cause gap: restoring only the last event's kind still lets `event_types` lose every other kind recorded before a restart. Matter's capability probe also maps only 'pin' out of `supported_credential_types`, so the vocabulary depends on use history rather than on what the lock reports it supports.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user unlocks with a card (`rfid`) and later with a PIN. After a restart the restored last kind is `pin`, so `rfid` disappears from `event_types` and the registry capabilities change until the next card tap. The general fix is to widen `event_types` from the lock's advertised supported kinds (Matter `supported_credential_types`) instead of special-casing the restore."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/event.py",
    "line": 223,
    "summary": "`async_get_last_event_data()` is called a second time right after `EventEntity.async_added_to_hass`, which has already read the same restore data into the entity.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every event entity, one per user per entry, does a redundant restore-store lookup and re-parses the stored data on each setup and reload. The restored type is already on the entity (it is what `state_attributes[ATTR_EVENT_TYPE]` publishes), so it can be read from there."
  },
  {
    "file": "blueprints/automation/lock_code_manager/slot_usage_limiter.yaml",
    "line": 5,
    "summary": "The blueprint's opening description still says it decrements 'each time a Lock Code Manager code slot PIN is used', which contradicts the paragraph this PR adds saying every kind of credential counts.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user reading the blueprint summary in the Home Assistant blueprint picker (the first sentence) concludes that card or fingerprint uses do not spend a use, then finds their limit used up by card taps."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 164,
    "summary": "New code comments use unexpanded acronyms ('RFID is the Door Lock cluster's own'; in matter.py `_kind_used`, 'the RFID reader', 'a keypad PIN'), against the project rule.",
    "failure_scenario": "Breaks AGENTS.md (the master guidance that /Users/raman/projects/lock_code_manager/CLAUDE.md tells agents to follow): '**Spell out acronyms** in code comments instead of using abbreviations.' The same PR spells out Near Field Communication, Bluetooth Low Energy and Ultra-Wideband but not Radio Frequency Identification or Personal Identification Number."
  }
]
```

