    "summary": "The LockOperation guard only drops userIndex below 1. An index the lock refuses (above NumberOfTotalUsersSupported, or a vendor index outside the user range) now sends a GetUser that the lock rejects, and the use is dropped instead of being found through the credentialIndex search.",
    "failure_scenario": "A lock reports a keypad unlock with userIndex 0xFFFE or one above max_users. GetUser is rejected (INVALID_COMMAND), _invoke_sdk raises LockDisconnected, and _dispatch_lock_operation logs at debug and returns, so the use is never announced. Before, the walk (bounded by nextUserIndex and max_users) never reached that index, found no match, and fell through to _lcm_slot_from_raw_users_by_credential_index, which announced the LCM slot. This is the same failure the PR fixes for index 0, only for the other end of the range."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1906,
    "summary": "Removed behavior: when the named user exists but is untagged, _lock_operation_slot now returns None without searching by credentialIndex. The old code ran that search whenever the userIndex lookup came back None.",
    "failure_scenario": "A lock whose LockOperation userIndex does not match the user actually holding the credential (a firmware that reports a different index, or a user re-created at a new index) names an untagged homeowner user, while the credential used belongs to an LCM-tagged user. The old code fell through and announced the LCM slot. The new code drops the use silently, and the only trace is a debug log saying it did not resolve."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 552,
    "summary": "_HELPER_MISMATCH (AttributeError/LookupError/TypeError/ValueError) around format_user(response) and endpoint_of(node) also catches errors caused by the lock's answer or a node mid-re-interview, not just a changed helper. Those are routed to the full walk, which hits the same error, and HA's get_lock_users raises it raw, outside _invoke_sdk's translation.",
    "failure_scenario": "A malformed GetUser answer makes _format_user_response raise TypeError, or _get_lock_endpoint_from_node raises KeyError while endpoints are rebuilt. This becomes _SingleUserReadUnavailable, and _raw_user_at calls _raw_lock_users. get_lock_users raises the same TypeError/KeyError, which is not a HomeAssistantError, so _invoke_sdk does not translate it. It escapes the `except LockOperationError` in _dispatch_lock_operation, _dispatch_lock_user_change and set/delete credential: an unhandled background-task exception, or a write that goes to the generic catch-all instead of the retry path."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1905,
    "summary": "When the single read is unavailable, _lock_operation_slot reads every user twice for a named user that is empty or not found: once in _raw_user_at's fallback walk, then again for the credentialIndex search.",
    "failure_scenario": "HA renames _format_user_response, and a keypad use names an empty index. _raw_user_at sends one GetUser per user and finds nothing, then _lcm_slot_from_raw_users_by_credential_index(await self._raw_lock_users(), ...) sends the whole walk again. On the 7-user Aqara from #1745 that is roughly double the ~8.6 s delay this PR sets out to remove. Reusing the first listing for the credential search would avoid it."
  },
  {
